SMP gate as a blocking wait: start the secondaries only after eMMC init.

0145 gated the secondary CPUs on onc_emmc_init() with a busy spin
(onc_spin_us) inside SweetCPU::startAll(), on a machine where only the boot
CPU was online.  That starved the bootstrap thread that calls
onc_emmc_init(), so the flag was never set and the ~120 s safety WDT reset
the SoC.  This time the gate is a real sleep.

Why gate at all: a secondary-CPU panic before onc_emmc_init() happens before
the Android BCB is cleared and before the SWEETLOG flusher starts, so the
reset boots the recovery partition again and the panic text only flashes on
the panel.  After the gate the BCB is already clear (reset -> boot=TWRP) and
the 0132 flusher writes the ring, panic text included, to the userdata tail.

  - SweetCPU.cpp: new C-linkage volatile int sweet_emmc_ready plus a one-shot
    gate inside the per-CPU start loop (skipped for the boot CPU): it blocks
    with assert_wait_timeout(10 ms) + thread_block() until the flag is set,
    up to sweetemmcgate=<secs> (default 60, 0 disables).  On timeout it logs
    and starts the secondaries anyway, so a broken gate still yields data
    instead of a hang.  No locks are held while waiting.
  - onc_emmc.c: onc_emmc_init() sets the flag and thread_wakeup()s after the
    0133 BCB clear and after the 0132 flusher start, printing
    "SWEET CPU: eMMC ready, SMP may start".

Expected: console up -> IOKit drivers -> "ONC EMMC: ... ready" ->
"SWEET LOG: console log to the last ..." -> "SWEET CPU: eMMC ready, SMP may
start" -> cpu=1 started -> "came alive; SGI1 OK" (or the timeout carrying
mark/sig/GICC).  Any panic now happens after the BCB clear, so the reset
returns to TWRP with the panic text on eMMC.

